## Worktree Workflow

All code changes in the snowflake repo use the `worktree` script (`~/.local/bin/worktree`).
The original clone is never modified directly.

### Layout

| Location | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `~/Projects/snowflake` | Read-only main clone (Mac, never edited) |
| `~/Projects/snowflake-worktree/worktree-<name>/` | Local worktree for a task (Mac) |
| `~/.local/share/worktree-meta/<name>.env` | Per-worktree metadata (REMOTE, BRANCH) |
| `<remote>:/home/repo/snowflake-worktree/main/code` | Remote main clone |
| `<remote>:/home/repo/snowflake-worktree/worktree-<name>/code` | Remote worktree checkout |
| `<remote>:/home/repo/snowflake-worktree/worktree-<name>/artifacts` | Remote build artifacts |
| `<remote>:/home/repo/snowflake` | Bind-mount point (active worktree's code) |
| `<remote>:~/sf` | Bind-mount point (active worktree's artifacts) |

### Standard workflow

```bash
# Start a new task (Mac)
worktree create <name> --remote <host> [--base <branch>]

# Push code and activate on remote — first time only
worktree deploy <name> --activate

# Iterate: commit locally, push to remote (SUT keeps running, no re-activate needed)
worktree deploy <name>

# Switch to a different worktree
worktree activate <other-name>

# Restore remote to main
worktree deactivate <name>

# Keep main up to date locally and on all remotes
worktree sync

# Clean up when done
worktree remove <name>
```

### Recovery

```bash
worktree force-reset <name>   # stop SUT, restore main, ignore lock
```

### Key rules
- Always work in a worktree — never edit `~/Projects/snowflake` directly
- `deploy` pushes commits; no re-activate needed after each push (bind mount already points there)
- `activate` is only needed on first deploy or when switching between worktrees
- Code cannot be built locally — deploy to remote and build there
